    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Is there a roadmap of work left to be done on lean4lean? It seems like it really should be better known by the community</p>",
        "id": 613849728,
        "sender_full_name": "nrs",
        "timestamp": 1785500951
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks for this find! Seems like I missed <code>Char.ofNat</code> in the List of declarations that our kernel supports natively when we moved off <code>List Char</code> based and to <code>ByteArray</code> based <code>String</code>. Great to see that the multiple implementation approach safed this one once again! I have a fix up at <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/comparator/pull/61\">https://github.com/leanprover/comparator/pull/61</a></p>\n<p>Regarding Lean4lean in comparator, originally my idea was that Lean's core kernel is just going to absorb the correctness relevant changes in Lean4lean so there should be no need to run it as you designed it to be deliberately similar to the core kernel. That said the idea of allowing users to just define a list of external kernels they wish to run their solution on is on my TODO stack for when i pop <code>bv_decide</code> off again.</p>",
        "id": 613853146,
        "sender_full_name": "Henrik Böving",
        "timestamp": 1785502213
    },
    {
        "content": "<blockquote>\n<p>Lean's core kernel is just going to absorb the correctness relevant changes in Lean4lean so there should be no need to run it as you designed it to be deliberately similar to the core kernel.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>If this was true, there would be no need for <code>divergences.md</code>. I'm currently trying to create soundness bugs from the entries documented there as a way to get people to merge my PRs</p>",
        "id": 613853372,
        "sender_full_name": "Mario Carneiro",
        "timestamp": 1785502284
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm aware of this yes, hence my change of mind <span aria-label=\"thumbs up\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f44d\" role=\"img\" title=\"thumbs up\">:thumbs_up:</span></p>",
        "id": 613853436,
        "sender_full_name": "Henrik Böving",
        "timestamp": 1785502309
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"749978\">nrs</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/270676-lean4/topic/Lean.2FComparator.20soundness.20bug.3A.20missing.20Char.2EofNat/near/613849728\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Is there a roadmap of work left to be done on lean4lean? It seems like it really should be better known by the community</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I'm not sure the opposite is an implicit assumption, but, to be clear, lean4lean is a volunteer project, not affiliated with the FRO. In fact, previous FRO interaction with lean4lean usually made lean4lean life harder, if I remember correctly (see, e.g., the alternate fix for <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/pull/8554\">lean4#8554</a>, or <a class=\"message-link\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/270676-lean4/topic/Counterexample.20to.20the.20Lean.20Conjecture.20.28Soundness.20Bug.29/near/613554939\">#lean4 &gt; Counterexample to the Lean Conjecture (Soundness Bug) @ 💬</a>).</p>",
        "id": 613853632,
        "sender_full_name": "Mauricio Collares",
        "timestamp": 1785502377
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>right, personally I know of lean4lean because of the papers published about metatheory but I was mainly asking/commenting because it seems like it is one possible solid solution to our current issues with static analysis. If we had a rough estimate about what kind of work needs to be done, maybe that could become a solid guidepost for what to aim for to keep a certain class of soundness bugs at bay?</p>",
        "id": 613854781,
        "sender_full_name": "nrs",
        "timestamp": 1785502795
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"110049\">@Mario Carneiro</span> when I look on <a href=\"https://comparator.live.lean-lang.org/#project=lean-v4.32.2&amp;challengez=C4Cwpg9gTmC2AEBLAdgYwsgzozwzOHgC54AxAQwBtMxiBeeAIwE8AoeeTaKZoA&amp;codez=FABwTgpgNgrgJhYBnCAXA%2BgexKglpgOwAIBzCAgYVUzAEk4APIgMwEMoVk0sd9ipcBVBDAA6dlBbtOwGASStmEIqwb4AtkSgBjAKJhWKOEQBcRACoBPEIjkKlKtZk06AggUumL12%2FMXLVDS1tADVMXGMzKxtgWQJcADcRFCIYWME4GG08JKIAMWllMwAFMGx0gkzsxOVzMBgIAHIvUuwiAHcACxFEIiIAHyJBVDKvOobGiqqc5QBVeNRG5q6e4D7B4dGzedxFyeAMrJmiADlWVA7uyDWBogAvEUwvM9QbwaQs7SIACmIzF4AlM9zrEALSgogAAwonVYYEhQyQKiISBGRxgkEumBSzFw0GMuCRrCIxQASgB5cl5IhU%2FKuAAyAGVdAAaFFPaGwsR%2FIgwuFEQBJhPlCpDRMBwbyuYjUvECLCAEa7CBwADcHWUBAgSTARHlMFwUGMhAgYtR9WyGOUfJ1UR8l1WfXUAGtTCYbjyChxEOKIZCAEKYBgIwnIs3ozHtXadFTEWgnAASrj9tHMugAIiw8YbTWiLZiA0xbTZ7ddHS6TG6%2BswxvUmlMjjUiPTCRdvoBG4DGdpgQJWpdu8UkZmbqKIbbeRG0hCR306kVHQO%2BqEHTZb868w4uY%2BQudQlqIjJGghIJd6REwzA3rpucHOxKHq%2BtYIh5njuiIAFlaIzGXGAOK818KAAaTMVBuiIJ0RE1SRNW1IhtVwXEICRTk4VEc8XmBC4hWtUUfSIAB1ZQEG0KA4WUXYiEgXcwFlY9%2FUDYN5GEVg4DFAhCEndQQBgVBWHlKBiIgatrXQ5hMN%2BLCgTMAtTAAXiIAtRGdCxa0aURNkwJ8iHUTAZjMSF3yIAciAUnY9khNlDJ%2BSd5CIURRCBBTcLZJAOW6ViEU1ZUkQ4qFcJYGgiDAjUDQnQxEA4gguJ4viBKE6sjO%2BZdL2taTvGLEw5JuAABDdREgL5rR%2BZg5CIdBTIAPgyiAgXMpYSrKirmqqyU4SBKBYiimLeP4wSiAQasPOMZL1wfLl0qMwdsr6PKWwKiAiqlIz6vUzTGuIWdyu2uTqtnDrtMhA8wCPUQbz4ogACIAB0GAABgAJkuhFwEwAArRbUCREKtCVAwoFEPpICqCBWjeogIAYEBWEqJFKOofC%2BmO07z0vb58ts6dRIw84iAeoF8oHAF8Jh4xqIxOyfoEVE2S6XBtGjSFBCUMAwYRVAfCJFDUq5PCJVoL6iGG6VIAkTxiQYoM2T1C4fsg2joGCzmhguQx%2FN59jOOcWK%2BoSidCFKowvGKrKhYgVifmRggSDO28rtux7LuJ%2FDUO5YMUNe8Hiruyz2RVoXSaReVWG0F0C0aJEkMNIgJQ5mxjDVyFPRQPCQpoCBNEETGW3IC4zGTopnK5URiFsw3lViABicBhkcDQ4eiqcc6EIA\">comparator live</a>, I see the failure happening <em>before</em> nanoda's double-check gets invoked, in the lean4export step, indicating it's a \"serialization format can't represent this\" bug. If it's only nanoda_lib leading to rejection, I'd expect to see a message that nanoda-lib checking had started.</p>",
